---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Back-of-the-envelope numbers" hint="How many chunks a file system has, how much memory the master needs for them, how much disk the replicas take, and how long it takes to copy a dead chunkserver's data again. A teaching model with round numbers, not Google's data.">
  <div class="pg est" data-est>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <RangeField label="Data stored, before copies (TB)" name="d" min="100" max="10000" step="100" value="1000" />
      <RangeField label="Chunk size (MB)" name="c" min="16" max="128" step="16" value="64" />
      <RangeField label="Copies of each chunk" name="r" min="2" max="5" value="3" />
      <RangeField label="Chunkservers" name="s" min="100" max="5000" step="100" value="1000" />
      <RangeField label="Master bytes per chunk" name="m" min="32" max="128" step="8" value="64" />
      <RangeField label="Clones running at once" name="n" min="10" max="200" step="10" value="91" />
      <RangeField label="Speed limit per clone (MB per second)" name="b" min="1" max="20" step="0.25" value="6.25" />
    </div>
    <dl class="est-out" data-out aria-live="polite"></dl>
    <p class="pg-note">Teaching model, not Google's system or data. From the GFS paper: chunks are 64 MB, three copies are the default, the master keeps less than 64 bytes of metadata per chunk, each 64 KB block has a 32-bit checksum, the largest clusters had over 1,000 storage nodes and over 300 TB, and in the paper's test 91 concurrent clones (40% of the chunkservers) at up to 6.25 MB per second each restored 600 GB in 23.2 minutes. The data size, the number of servers and the other values are my guesses that you can change. Assumptions: one terabyte is 1,000,000 MB, chunks and copies are spread evenly, every clone runs at its limit, and the master's other metadata (the namespace, locations, versions) is ignored. The paper's measured time is longer than the limit alone predicts because clones do not always run at full speed.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue, formatOneDecimalBelow100 } from '../../playground';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-est]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = numberValue(q);
    const OUT_ROWS = 10; // lines in the result list
    const rows = Array.from({ length: OUT_ROWS }, () => { const dt = document.createElement('dt'); const dd = document.createElement('dd'); q('[data-out]').append(dt, dd); return { dt, dd }; });
    const f1 = formatOneDecimalBelow100;
    const pct = (n: number) => `${(Math.round(n * 1000) / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')}%`;
    function draw() {
      ['d', 'c', 'r', 's', 'm', 'n', 'b'].forEach((key) => (q(`[data-${key}val]`).textContent = String(val(`[data-${key}]`))));
      const tb = val('[data-d]'); const chunkMb = val('[data-c]'); const copies = val('[data-r]'); const servers = val('[data-s]');
      const metaBytes = val('[data-m]'); const clones = val('[data-n]'); const speed = val('[data-b]');
      const chunks = (tb * 1_000_000) / chunkMb; const rawTb = tb * copies; const perServerTb = rawTb / servers;
      const cloneRate = clones * speed; const heal = (perServerTb * 1_000_000) / cloneRate;
      const data: [string, string][] = [
        ['Chunks in the file system (millions)', f1(chunks / 1e6)],
        ['Master memory for chunk metadata (GB)', f1((chunks * metaBytes) / 1e9)],
        ['Disk used with all copies (TB)', f1(rawTb)],
        ['Data on each chunkserver (TB)', f1(perServerTb)],
        ['Chunks on each chunkserver', f1((chunks * copies) / servers)],
        ['Checksum bytes for all copies (GB)', f1((rawTb * 1_000_000 * 4) / 0.064 / 1e9)],
        ['Master requests to read 1 TB in sequence, with cold caches', f1(1_000_000 / chunkMb)],
        ['Copying speed with all clones running (MB per second)', f1(cloneRate)],
        ['Minutes to copy a dead chunkserver\u2019s data again', f1(heal / 60)],
        ['Chunkserver failures the data survives at once', String(copies - 1)],
      ];
      rows.forEach((r, i) => { r.dt.textContent = data[i][0]; r.dd.textContent = data[i][1]; });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
